    21	### 1. The Provenance Envelope
    22	
    23	```rust
    24	pub struct Provenance {
    25	    // Envelope schema version — for forward-compatibility on this ADR's structure itself
    26	    pub provenance_schema_version: u32,
    27	    
    28	    // Identity
    29	    pub ability_name: &'static str,
    30	    pub ability_version: AbilityVersion,
    31	    pub ability_schema_version: SchemaVersion,   // The ability's I/O schema version, per ADR-0102 §8
    32	    pub invocation_id: InvocationId,
    33	    
    34	    // Temporal context
    35	    pub produced_at: DateTime<Utc>,              // From ctx.services.clock.now() per ADR-0104 §6
    36	    pub inputs_snapshot: InputsSnapshot,          // See §2
    37	    
    38	    // Execution context
    39	    pub actor: Actor,
    40	    pub mode: ExecutionMode,
    41	    
    42	    // Trust classification
    43	    pub trust: TrustAssessment,                   // See §3
    44	    
    45	    // Source attribution — directly cited sources (transitive sources live under children[])
    46	    pub sources: Vec<SourceAttribution>,
    47	    
    48	    // Prompt fingerprint — populated when IntelligenceProvider was invoked. Shape per ADR-0106.
    49	    pub prompt_fingerprint: Option<PromptFingerprint>,
    50	    
    51	    // Composition tree — child provenance from composed abilities
    52	    pub children: Vec<Provenance>,
    53	    
    54	    // Field-level attribution (required for LLM-synthesized fields; see §5)
    55	    pub field_attributions: BTreeMap<FieldPath, FieldAttribution>,
    56	    
    57	    // Diagnostics
    58	    pub warnings: Vec<ProvenanceWarning>,
    59	}
    60	
    61	pub enum ProvenanceWarning {
    62	    DepthElided { skipped_levels: u32 },
    63	    SourceStale { source_index: usize, age: Duration },
    64	    SourceUnresolvable { source_index: usize, reason: String },
    65	    AttributionIncomplete { field: FieldPath },
    66	    Masked { reason: MaskReason },
    67	}
    68	```
    69	
    70	`provenance_schema_version` is the schema version of this ADR's envelope structure. A consumer seeing a higher version must parse forward-compatibly — unknown fields are ignored; known fields retain their meaning. The initial version is `1`. Any breaking change requires a new version and a migration path.
    71	
    72	`ability_schema_version` is the ability's I/O schema version per [ADR-0102](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md) §8 — a different concept, retained here for completeness so a consumer reading provenance knows exactly which ability contract produced the output.
    73	
    74	### 2. Inputs Snapshot
    75	
    76	Reproducibility requires a precise description of what the ability saw when it ran. The `InputsSnapshot` captures this beyond just signals:
    77	
    78	```rust
    79	pub struct InputsSnapshot {
    80	    pub newest_signal_at: Option<DateTime<Utc>>,    // Max observed_at across signal inputs
    81	    pub entity_watermarks: BTreeMap<EntityId, EntityWatermark>,  // Version + last_updated per entity read
    82	    pub source_freshness: BTreeMap<SourceClass, DateTime<Utc>>,  // Per-source last-sync timestamps
    83	    pub provider_config_hash: Hash,                  // Hash of provider settings at invocation time
    84	    pub glean_connected: bool,                       // Per ADR-0100
    85	}
    86	
    87	pub struct EntityWatermark {
    88	    pub entity_version: u64,
    89	    pub last_updated: DateTime<Utc>,
    90	}
    91	```
    92	
    93	This does not claim to enable exact content-level replay of deleted or mutated sources. It supports three legitimate use cases:
    94	
    95	1. **Staleness detection.** A consumer renders "as of 14:23 UTC, freshest Glean sync 3 hours ago."
    96	2. **Diff reasoning.** A second invocation with different watermarks shows what changed.
    97	3. **Eval reproducibility under fixtures.** In `Evaluate` mode, the fixture's state is deterministic; the snapshot plus the fixture reconstructs the run. [ADR-0110](0110-evaluation-harness-for-abilities.md) (forthcoming — renumbered from 0107) specifies how.
    98	
    99	**Reproducibility is a scoped claim, not an unqualified one.** Full content-level replay of non-fixture runs is not promised and is out of scope for this ADR.
   100	
   101	### 3. Trust Assessment
   102	
   103	Trust is a structured record, not a collapsed nested enum. It encodes the weakest trust encountered, the mixing history, and whether the output is safe to authorize mutation on without a trust signal per [ADR-0102](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md) §10.
   104	
   105	```rust
   106	pub struct TrustAssessment {
   107	    /// The effective trust of this output for mutation-authorization purposes.
   108	    pub effective: EffectiveTrust,
   109	    
   110	    /// Contributions to the trust computation. Lists every ancestor whose
   111	    /// output mixed into this one, annotated with why its trust class applies.
   112	    pub contributions: Vec<TrustContribution>,
   113	    
   114	    /// True if any ancestor (Read or otherwise) returned content that was
   115	    /// originally LLM-synthesized and stored, even if this ability's direct
   116	    /// category is Read. Closes the "stored AI output laundering" hole.
   117	    pub contains_stored_synthesis: bool,
   118	}
   119	
   120	pub enum EffectiveTrust {
   121	    /// Safe to use for mutation authorization without a trust signal.
   122	    Trusted,
   123	    
   124	    /// Must not be used for mutation authorization without confirmation,
   125	    /// policy, or schema-bounded value per ADR-0102 §10.
   126	    Untrusted,
   127	}
   128	
   129	pub struct TrustContribution {
   130	    pub source: TrustContributionSource,
   131	    pub reason: TrustReason,
   132	}
   133	
   134	pub enum TrustContributionSource {
   135	    DirectSource { index: usize },         // Points into Provenance.sources
   136	    ComposedChild { index: usize },        // Points into Provenance.children
   137	    StoredSynthesisField { entity_id: EntityId, field: String },
   138	}
   139	
   140	pub enum TrustReason {
   141	    DirectlyFromStructuredSource,          // User-typed, Salesforce field, calendar event attribute
   142	    DirectlyFromLLMSynthesis,              // This ability invoked IntelligenceProvider
   143	    ComposedUntrustedChild,                // Child ability returned Untrusted
   144	    StoredSynthesis,                       // Read ability exposed a stored LLM-synthesized value
   145	    UnboundedFreeText,                     // External text that was not schema-bounded
   146	}
   147	```
   148	
   149	**Rules for computing `effective`:**
   150	
   151	1. If `prompt_fingerprint.is_some()`, this ability invoked the LLM → `Untrusted`.
   152	2. If any `contributions[i]` has `reason == ComposedUntrustedChild` or `DirectlyFromLLMSynthesis` or `StoredSynthesis` or `UnboundedFreeText`, the output is `Untrusted`.
   153	3. If every contribution is `DirectlyFromStructuredSource`, the output is `Trusted`.
   154	4. `contains_stored_synthesis` is true when any source is a stored field known to have been LLM-synthesized (per [ADR-0107](0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md), stored fields carry a `synthesized_by` marker so this can be detected at read time).
   155	
   156	**This closes the laundering hole.** A Read ability that returns stored AI output (e.g., a cached `entity_assessment.summary` from Glean) is `Untrusted` because the stored field itself carries a synthesis marker. The `contains_stored_synthesis` flag and `TrustReason::StoredSynthesis` contribution make the reasoning explicit.
   157	
   158	**`effective` is computed by the provenance builder, not the author.** The author cannot set it; the registry rejects manually-constructed `Provenance` with mismatched `effective` and `contributions`.
   159	
   160	### 4. Source Attribution
   161	
   162	Source attribution references the authoritative `DataSource` taxonomy in [ADR-0107](0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md). This ADR specifies the shape; that ADR specifies the enum values and lifecycle behavior.
   163	
   164	```rust
   165	pub struct SourceAttribution {
   166	    pub data_source: DataSource,                 // Defined by ADR-0107
   167	    pub identifiers: Vec<SourceIdentifier>,      // Defined by ADR-0107 (extensible enum)
   168	    pub observed_at: DateTime<Utc>,              // When DailyOS last ingested this source
   169	    pub source_asof: Option<DateTime<Utc>>,      // When the source itself was authored (if known)
   170	    pub evidence_weight: f32,                    // How strongly this source contributed, [0.0, 1.0]
   171	    pub scoring_class: ScoringClass,             // See below
   172	    pub synthesis_marker: Option<SynthesisMarker>, // Set if this source is itself stored LLM synthesis
   173	}
   174	
   175	pub enum ScoringClass {
   176	    /// Source contributes to numeric scoring (health, risk, etc.). Example: Salesforce field.
   177	    Scoring,
   178	    
   179	    /// Source is context-only, never feeds numeric scoring. Per ADR-0100, Slack and P2 are Context.
   180	    Context,
   181	    
   182	    /// Source is reference material for LLM synthesis, not direct attribution.
   183	    Reference,
   184	}
   185	
   186	pub struct SynthesisMarker {
   187	    pub producer_ability: &'static str,
   188	    pub producer_invocation_id: InvocationId,
   189	    pub produced_at: DateTime<Utc>,
   190	}
   191	```
   192	
   193	**`evidence_weight` is a contribution weight, not a reliability score.** It is author-supplied (by the ability's planner or synthesizer) and indicates how much this particular source mattered for this specific output. It is not a stored per-source reliability — those are defined by [ADR-0080](0080-signal-intelligence-architecture.md) and consumed separately. We deliberately do not duplicate ADR-0080's reliability into every provenance envelope.
   194	
   195	**`scoring_class` is derived from the `DataSource` per [ADR-0107](0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md).** It is informational, so consumers can filter context-only sources from scoring analysis.
   196	
   197	**Glean-sourced attribution for invisible content.** When Glean returns an assessment computed from sources DailyOS cannot locally see (e.g., Zendesk tickets the user's Glean account can read but DailyOS cannot), the ability attributes `data_source: Glean` with a `SourceIdentifier::GleanAssessment { assessment_id, cited_sources: Vec<GleanCitedSource> }`. Lifecycle of these invisible sources is governed by the user's Glean policy, not DailyOS's local lifecycle. Details in [ADR-0107](0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md).
   198	
   199	### 5. Field-Level Attribution
   200	
   201	Every field in the ability's output is attributable to one or more sources or children:
   202	
   203	```rust
   204	pub struct FieldPath(String);  // JSON-pointer-like, e.g. "/topics/0/title"
   205	
   206	pub struct FieldAttribution {
   207	    pub derivation: DerivationKind,
   208	    pub source_refs: Vec<SourceRef>,
   209	    pub confidence: Confidence,
   210	    pub explanation: Option<SanitizedExplanation>,  // Sanitization rules in ADR-0108
   211	}
   212	
   213	pub enum DerivationKind {
   214	    Direct,                                        // Copied from a single source identifier
   215	    Composed { child_idx: usize },                 // From a composed child's output
   216	    Computed { algorithm: &'static str },          // Algorithmic from structured sources
   217	    LLMSynthesis,                                  // Produced by the LLM given the listed sources
   218	    Constant,                                      // Fixed value, not derived
   219	}
   220	
   221	pub struct Confidence {
   222	    pub value: f32,                                // [0.0, 1.0]
   223	    pub kind: ConfidenceKind,                      // How the value was obtained
   224	}
   225	
   226	pub enum ConfidenceKind {
   227	    Declared,              // Author-set (must justify in explanation)
   228	    ProviderReported,      // Returned by IntelligenceProvider
   229	    ComposedMin,           // Min of composed children's confidences
   230	    Computed,              // From algorithm
   231	    Implicit,              // Direct copy is always 1.0 (trivially true)
   232	}
   233	```
   234	
   235	**Rules:**
   236	
   237	1. Every field in the ability's domain output MUST have an entry in `field_attributions`. No "optional for Read" escape hatch — Read abilities' fields are typically `Direct` with `ConfidenceKind::Implicit`, which is trivial to populate but must exist.
   238	2. `LLMSynthesis` derivation requires non-empty `source_refs`. An LLM-synthesized field with zero sources is a registration error.
   239	3. `Declared` confidence requires a non-empty `explanation` explaining the basis. The builder rejects `Declared` without explanation.
   240	4. `Confidence.value == 1.0` is not a bypass for attribution. Direct copies are 1.0 and still have an attribution entry (it's trivial, but it exists). LLM synthesis with declared 1.0 requires an explanation.
   241	5. `FieldPath` uses JSON-Pointer syntax with a stability caveat: array-index paths (`/topics/0`) are tolerated for render-time display but are not stable across re-runs. Stable identifiers (`/topics/[id=abc123]/title`) SHOULD be used when the output structure contains stable IDs.
   242	
   243	### 6. Composition and Merge Semantics
   244	
   245	When ability A invokes ability B, A's provenance includes B's as a nested `child`:
   246	
   247	```
   248	Provenance(A) {
   249	  sources:  [... A's direct sources ...],
   250	  children: [Provenance(B), Provenance(C), ...],
   251	  field_attributions: {
   252	    "/some_field": FieldAttribution { derivation: Composed { child_idx: 0 }, ... }
   253	  }
   254	}
   255	```
   256	
   257	**Merge rules:**
   258	
   259	1. `sources[]` lists the ability's *own* direct sources; transitive sources live under `children[]`. The tree stays sparse.
   260	2. `trust.effective` merges bottom-up per the rules in §3.
   261	3. `prompt_fingerprint` does not merge — each ability has its own or none.
   262	4. `inputs_snapshot` is the ability's own view at invocation start. Children capture their own snapshots; no aggregation.
   263	5. Deduplication across `children[i].sources[]` is render-time, not construction-time.
   264	6. Cycle guard: the registry rejects ability composition cycles at registration time per [ADR-0102](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md) §11.1, so nested `Provenance` trees are guaranteed acyclic.
   265	
   266	**`SourceRef::Child(child_idx, field_path)` uses a stable `composition_id`.** To avoid the brittleness of positional `child_idx`, the `children` vector is indexed by a stable `CompositionId` per declared `composes` entry in the ability's metadata (per [ADR-0102](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md) §7.1). The `child_idx` is the index into the `composes` list, not the runtime invocation order. A `SourceRef::Child(child_idx, field_path)` with a `child_idx` outside the declared `composes` list is a registration error.
   267	
   268	### 7. Provenance Builder Pattern
   269	
   270	Contributors produce provenance via a builder that enforces the rules in §3 and §5:
   271	
   272	```rust
   273	pub async fn prepare_meeting(
   274	    ctx: &AbilityContext<'_>,
   275	    input: PrepareMeetingInput,
   276	) -> AbilityResult<MeetingBrief> {
   277	    let mut prov = ProvenanceBuilder::new(ctx, "prepare_meeting");
   278	    
   279	    // Structured source (Trusted)
   280	    let meeting = ctx.services.meetings.get(input.meeting_id).await?;
   281	    let meeting_src = prov.source(
   282	        DataSource::Google,
   283	        SourceIdentifier::Meeting { meeting_id: meeting.id },
   284	        ScoringClass::Scoring,
   285	    );
   286	    
   287	    // Composed Read ability
   288	    let account_ctx = ctx.invoke_typed(get_entity_context, GetEntityContextInput { ... }).await?;
   289	    let account_child_idx = prov.compose(&account_ctx.provenance);
   290	    
   291	    // LLM synthesis — builder enforces that this triggers Untrusted trust
   292	    let completion = ctx.intelligence.complete(prompt, ModelTier::Synthesis).await?;
   293	    prov.prompt_fingerprint(completion.fingerprint);  // Shape per ADR-0106
   294	    
   295	    // Explicit attributions
   296	    prov.attribute("/topics", DerivationKind::LLMSynthesis, &[
   297	        SourceRef::Source(meeting_src),
   298	        SourceRef::Child(account_child_idx, "/entity_state".into()),
   299	    ], Confidence::provider_reported(completion.confidence));
   300	    
   301	    Ok(prov.finalize(MeetingBrief { topics: completion.topics, ... }))
   302	}
   303	```
   304	
   305	The builder:
   306	
   307	- Auto-populates `field_attributions` for direct projections (`pass_through` helper).
   308	- Computes `trust.effective` from sources and composition.
   309	- Rejects manual overrides to computed fields (`trust.effective`, `contains_stored_synthesis`).
   310	- Wraps the domain output in `AbilityOutput<T>` with the built provenance.
   311	- Fails at build time if any output field lacks attribution.
   312	
   313	### 8. Storage Integration
   314	
   315	Provenance persists anywhere the ability output is stored. Specifically:
   316	
   317	1. **Maintenance audit table.** [ADR-0103](0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md) §8 `MaintenanceAuditRecord` adds a `provenance: Provenance` field. Source revocation triggers a masking pass: a `Provenance` whose sources reference a revoked `DataSource` is replaced with a `ProvenanceMasked` marker per [ADR-0108](0108-provenance-rendering-and-privacy.md). **Interaction with [ADR-0094](0094-audit-log-and-enterprise-observability.md):** ADR-0094's append-only JSONL security audit remains unchanged. The `maintenance_audit` SQLite table is a separate operational log for runtime diagnostics. Provenance content in the operational log is subject to masking; the JSONL security log is unchanged.
   318	
   319	2. **Outbox table.** [ADR-0103](0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md) §2 outbox entries add a `provenance: Provenance` field. Outbox provenance captures the ability's full output-time provenance, including the invocation that generated the pending external mutation.
   320	
   321	3. **Planned mutations.** [ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md) §3 `PlannedMutation` adds a `provenance_ref: InvocationId` field pointing into the ability's provenance envelope. Plans do not carry a full duplicated provenance; they reference the envelope by invocation ID and field path, preventing storage duplication.
   322	
   323	4. **Source revocation behavior.** Revoking a source invalidates derived artifacts per [ADR-0107](0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md)'s lifecycle rules, which may be "purge" (destructive) or "flag for re-enrichment" (preservation) depending on the source class. ADR-0105 defines how masking presents to consumers; ADR-0107 defines when purge vs. flag applies.
   324	
   325	**Storage format.** Provenance is stored as JSON in a single column per containing record. Indexes on `invocation_id`, `ability_name`, `produced_at`, and `data_source` (via a normalized xref table) support the queries [ADR-0103](0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md) §8 requires. A full JSON-column approach is feasible at the six-user scale; if scale changes, a normalized `provenance` / `provenance_sources` split can be introduced without changing the envelope contract.
   326	
   327	### 9. Size Budget and Truncation
   328	
   329	A typical Read ability produces ~500B–5KB of provenance. Transform abilities with field attributions can reach ~50KB. Deeply composed Maintenance invocations with >8 composition levels or >50 sources can exceed 500KB, which is excessive for per-invocation storage.
   330	
   331	**Soft budget.** Abilities SHOULD keep provenance under 100KB. The registry warns at build time when provenance exceeds the soft budget.
   332	
   333	**Hard truncation.** When a provenance envelope exceeds 1MB at finalize time, composition subtrees are collapsed from deepest first into a `ProvenanceWarning::DepthElided` summary carrying aggregate source counts and trust classification. Truncation is a finalize-time concern; the full tree never leaves the ability in its raw form above the hard limit.
   334	
   335	## Consequences
   336	
   337	### Positive
   338	
   339	1. **Explainability is structural.** Every output answers "where did this come from?" without additional instrumentation.
   340	2. **Trust boundary is enforceable and closes the laundering hole.** [ADR-0102](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md) §10 rejects mutations authorized by `Untrusted` output at the registry layer, and `contains_stored_synthesis` catches the case where stored AI output is surfaced by a Read ability.

